The backend to exec is this very executable; do not go looking for it.

Under EXEC_BACKEND the postmaster resolves the binary it will exec for each
child with find_other_exec(), which locates a `postgres` beside argv[0] and then
version-checks it by running `"<path>" -V` through popen(). That is a /bin/sh
and a second postgres -- two complete process lifetimes -- before the server has
done any work, and it is the only reason this image needs a shell at all.

The check is worth having on a general-purpose system, where the binary next
door may belong to a different installation. In a unikernel the root filesystem
is part of the image: there is exactly one postgres, it is the one already
running, and nothing can have replaced it since boot.

--- a/src/backend/postmaster/postmaster.c
+++ b/src/backend/postmaster/postmaster.c
@@ -1536,12 +1536,21 @@
 				(errmsg("%s: could not locate my own executable path", argv0)));
 
 #ifdef EXEC_BACKEND
-	/* Locate executable backend before we change working directory */
-	if (find_other_exec(argv0, "postgres", PG_BACKEND_VERSIONSTR,
-						postgres_exec_path) < 0)
-		ereport(FATAL,
-				(errmsg("%s: could not locate matching postgres executable",
-						argv0)));
+	/*
+	 * The backend to exec is this very executable.
+	 *
+	 * find_other_exec() would look for a `postgres` beside argv[0] and then
+	 * version-check it by running `"<path>" -V` through popen() -- which means
+	 * a /bin/sh and a second postgres, two whole process lifetimes, before the
+	 * server has done anything. That check earns its keep on a general-purpose
+	 * system, where the binary next door can be from another installation. In
+	 * a unikernel the root filesystem is part of the image: there is exactly
+	 * one postgres, it is the one running, and it cannot have been upgraded
+	 * underneath us.
+	 *
+	 * my_exec_path was just resolved above, so use it.
+	 */
+	strlcpy(postgres_exec_path, my_exec_path, MAXPGPATH);
 #endif
 
 	/*
